00:44:57,250 --> 00:45:01,124
through the bitter, harsh,
cold, unfeeling exterior

1005
00:45:01,125 --> 00:45:03,290
- to the--
- Ha-ha. Just kidding. Just kidding.

1006
00:45:03,291 --> 00:45:05,207
- To the soft, squishy center.
- Yes.

1007
00:45:05,208 --> 00:45:07,582
- Yeah.
- Okay, so how did you two get together?

1008
00:45:07,583 --> 00:45:09,582
I would love to hear this story.

1009
00:45:09,583 --> 00:45:11,915
Oh, it was not really that interesting.

1010
00:45:11,916 --> 00:45:13,207
I, uh... I asked her out

1011
00:45:13,208 --> 00:45:14,832
- for a cup of coffee and--
- No.

1012
00:45:14,833 --> 00:45:16,415
- There's more to it than that.
- No? More than coffee?

1013
00:45:16,416 --> 00:45:18,665
Yeah. Should I tell them?
Don't be embarrassed. It's funny.

1014
00:45:18,666 --> 00:45:19,749
- Really?
- Yeah.

1015
00:45:19,750 --> 00:45:21,708
- I think we'd all love to hear it, right?
- Yeah.

1016
00:45:22,458 --> 00:45:24,790
I was working late one night in the lab,

1017
00:45:24,791 --> 00:45:28,540
and then I, um... I heard this screaming
coming from Adam's office,

1018
00:45:28,541 --> 00:45:31,040
so I... I rush down the hallway,
I look in there,

1019
00:45:31,041 --> 00:45:33,958
and there he is, he's shrieking.

1020
00:45:34,833 --> 00:45:37,499
- I don't think I was shrieking, Olive.
- Oh, he was shrieking.

1021
00:45:37,500 --> 00:45:39,249
- Was I shriek-- Really?
- Yes.

1022
00:45:39,250 --> 00:45:42,790
And he was, like, crouched down in
the corner, like, frightened and shaking

1023
00:45:42,791 --> 00:45:44,582
because there was a spider on his desk.

1024
00:45:44,583 --> 00:45:45,999
No.

1025
00:45:46,000 --> 00:45:48,332
- Spider?
- Adam, you're afraid of spiders?

1026
00:45:48,333 --> 00:45:50,040
- No, I'm not afraid of spiders.
- He is.

1027
00:45:50,041 --> 00:45:51,832
No, no, um, I think it was more of a yell

1028
00:45:51,833 --> 00:45:53,624
- of surprise than--
- No, it was pure fear.

1029
00:45:53,625 --> 00:45:55,249
- Pure fear? Wow.
- He's absolutely terrified of them.

1030
00:45:55,250 --> 00:45:58,207
He... he even sleeps
with a little silk cap so they don't crawl

1031
00:45:58,208 --> 00:45:59,499
- into your hair at night.
- Well, that is ridiculous.

1032
00:45:59,500 --> 00:46:00,457
- No, it's--
- Isn't it?

1033
00:46:00,458 --> 00:46:01,457
- Don't you think that's--
- Well, you do it.

1034
00:46:01,458 --> 00:46:04,082
- People have phobias, babe. It's fine.
- Right.

1035
00:46:04,083 --> 00:46:06,749
I caught it for him. I let it go outside.

1036
00:46:06,750 --> 00:46:08,540
It was not... [whispers] very big.

1037
00:46:08,541 --> 00:46:10,124
And then I came back in,

1038
00:46:10,125 --> 00:46:11,582
- and he wiped his tears...
- Mm-hmm.

1039
00:46:11,583 --> 00:46:14,457
...and then asked me out for coffee
to thank me for saving him.

1040
00:46:14,458 --> 00:46:16,665
Wow. Brave and smart.

1041
00:46:16,666 --> 00:46:19,165
- You really hit the jackpot, my friend.
- I did. Yes.

1042
00:46:19,166 --> 00:46:21,790
She's very... very creative.

1043
00:46:21,791 --> 00:46:23,415
- Mm-hmm.
- Speaking of creative,

1044
00:46:23,416 --> 00:46:25,457
I'd love to hear more about your project.

1045
00:46:25,458 --> 00:46:28,124
Ah, come on, Tom.
It's a party, not a chalk talk.

1046
00:46:28,125 --> 00:46:29,707
Let her just enjoy herself.

1047
00:46:29,708 --> 00:46:31,957
We're surrounded by the brightest
and best minds in the industry here.

1048
00:46:31,958 --> 00:46:34,083
I mean,
what else are we gonna talk about?

1049
00:46:35,291 --> 00:46:37,666
Olive, you have the floor.

1050
00:46:38,916 --> 00:46:40,583
- Please.
- Yeah. Go for it.

1051
00:46:41,250 --> 00:46:43,666
Everyone...
Hey, kill that music.

1052
00:46:47,833 --> 00:46:48,958
Hello.

1053
00:46:49,958 --> 00:46:52,083
Um, I...

1054
00:46:54,541 --> 00:46:58,040
Okay. Uh, so...

1055
00:46:58,041 --> 00:47:02,124
Pancreatic cancer is one of
the most aggressive, deadliest cancers,

1056
00:47:02,125 --> 00:47:06,082
with very poor prognosis,
and early detection is key,

1057
00:47:06,083 --> 00:47:09,832
but it is incredibly hard
to diagnose at curable phase.

1058
00:47:09,833 --> 00:47:11,999
But we do have very effective
screening tools,

1059
00:47:12,000 --> 00:47:13,832
like magnetic resonance imaging,

1060
00:47:13,833 --> 00:47:16,374
that have proven quite successful
for diagnosis.

1061
00:47:16,375 --> 00:47:18,582
Yes, but-but that is expensive

1062
00:47:18,583 --> 00:47:22,290
and often not useful,
due to the position of the pancreas.

1063
00:47:22,291 --> 00:47:25,332
But biomarkers, specifically blood--

1064
00:47:25,333 --> 00:47:27,540
This is well-worn territory.

1065
00:47:27,541 --> 00:47:32,207
Multiple translational research studies
have explored noninvasive biomarkers,

1066
00:47:32,208 --> 00:47:36,416
and none, alone or in combination,
are superior to CA 19-9.

1067
00:47:37,166 --> 00:47:41,499
True, yes, but, um,
CA 19-9 can be unreliable

1068
00:47:41,500 --> 00:47:44,207
due to its lack of expression
in five percent of the population--

1069
00:47:44,208 --> 00:47:45,415
Unreliable?

1070
00:47:45,416 --> 00:47:46,624
Ridiculous.

1071
00:47:46,625 --> 00:47:50,374
Nothing even comes close to it
in terms of sensitivity and specificity.

1072
00:47:50,375 --> 00:47:52,166
Why pancreatic cancer?

1073
00:47:53,250 --> 00:47:54,249
Sorry?

1074
00:47:54,250 --> 00:47:57,707
Uh, look, that's actually irrelevant
and, honestly, invasive.

1075
00:47:57,708 --> 00:47:59,707
Olive, you don't have to speak to that.

1076
00:47:59,708 --> 00:48:01,832
Well, personal connection
is what separates

1077
00:48:01,833 --> 00:48:04,665
the good scientists from the great,
wouldn't you say?

1078
00:48:04,666 --> 00:48:07,040
You really think you need to have
a personal connection

1079
00:48:07,041 --> 00:48:09,624
to want to cure cancer, Tom?

1080
00:48:09,625 --> 00:48:12,624
No, there-there's...
there's no personal connection.

1081
00:48:12,625 --> 00:48:15,624
I just, um, enjoy being challenged.

1082
00:48:15,625 --> 00:48:18,624
Well, I hope you also enjoy
being disappointed.

1083
00:48:18,625 --> 00:48:20,625
Harsh.

1084
00:48:27,458 --> 00:48:29,166
I'm gonna use the bathroom.

1085
00:48:33,958 --> 00:48:35,749
Nice.

1086
00:48:35,750 --> 00:48:37,583
- Excuse me.
- Oh.

1087
00:48:44,500 --> 00:48:46,082
Olive. Olive, wait.

1088
00:48:46,083 --> 00:48:48,249
- Where are you going?
- No, I'm going home. I'm sorry.

1089
00:48:48,250 --> 00:48:50,124
Okay, well, let me take you.

1090
00:48:50,125 --> 00:48:52,457
- No, stay with your friends.
- They're not my friends.

1091
00:48:52,458 --> 00:48:55,082
They're professional colleagues
that I have to interact with.

1092
00:48:55,083 --> 00:48:58,124
- Plea-- Can you just let me drive you?
- No, I want to walk. It's not far.

1093
00:48:58,125 --> 00:49:00,374
Go back to your cocktail party. I'm fine.

1094
00:49:00,375 --> 00:49:02,124
I'm not gonna let you walk home alone.

1095
00:49:02,125 --> 00:49:04,790
- I don't want to talk, Adam.
- Great.

1096
00:49:04,791 --> 00:49:06,415
I hardly ever want to talk.

1097
00:49:06,416 --> 00:49:08,332
To anyone.

1098
00:49:08,333 --> 00:49:10,624
I sounded like I didn't know
what I was talking about.

1099
00:49:10,625 --> 00:49:12,165
- No, you didn't.
- No, yes, I did.

1100
00:49:12,166 --> 00:49:15,874
I know what I want to say.
I can't... say it.

1101
00:49:15,875 --> 00:49:18,750
- I get overwhelmed. I sweat.
- Well, you can't tell.

1102
00:49:20,166 --> 00:49:22,499
Okay. Wow. That is a lot of, um...

1103
00:49:22,500 --> 00:49:24,624
But, look, you told
the spider story really well.

1104
00:49:24,625 --> 00:49:26,082
It was very charming.

1105
00:49:26,083 --> 00:49:27,832
That's different.
That doesn't mean anything.

1106
00:49:27,833 --> 00:49:28,999
I was just taunting you.

1107
00:49:29,000 --> 00:49:31,040
Yeah, no, I got that. Thank you.

1108
00:49:31,041 --> 00:49:33,915
Oh, Dr. Keeney,
"I hope you enjoy being disappointed."

1109
00:49:33,916 --> 00:49:35,707
Yeah, I'm addicted to it.

1110
00:49:35,708 --> 00:49:37,082
That's why I'm a woman in science.

1111
00:49:37,083 --> 00:49:38,999
See, you just need to be more like Keeney.

1112
00:49:39,000 --> 00:49:41,833
Carry yourself with the confidence
of a very mediocre white man.

1113
00:49:45,958 --> 00:49:48,082
- I'm not crying, by the way.
- Oh, I know.

1114
00:49:48,083 --> 00:49:49,832
They're those expired contacts.

1115
00:49:49,833 --> 00:49:51,833
Yeah, I really need to--

1116
00:49:54,333 --> 00:49:56,415
That was you?

1117
00:49:56,416 --> 00:49:58,375
In the lab bathroom two years ago?

1118
00:49:59,500 --> 00:50:00,999
Wow, you really are blind.

1119
00:50:01,000 --> 00:50:03,458
I can't believe that was you.

1120
00:50:05,041 --> 00:50:09,124
You really calmed me down before
the most important interview of my life.

1121
00:50:09,125 --> 00:50:11,291
You belong here. You just had to hear it.

1122
00:50:13,875 --> 00:50:15,166
Um...

1123
00:50:16,875 --> 00:50:19,082
You know, for the optics.

1124
00:50:19,083 --> 00:50:20,875
- Right?
- Right.

1125
00:50:23,916 --> 00:50:25,915
I lied to everyone

1126
00:50:25,916 --> 00:50:29,957
about my personal connection
to the research.

1127
00:50:29,958 --> 00:50:31,790
I-I do.

1128
00:50:31,791 --> 00:50:33,083
I know.

1129
00:50:34,666 --> 00:50:37,124
You're passionate and incredibly driven.

1130
00:50:37,125 --> 00:50:39,458
That comes from somewhere.

1131
00:50:45,791 --> 00:50:49,625
My mom died from pancreatic cancer
when I was 15.

1132
00:50:51,750 --> 00:50:53,416
I am so sorry.

1133
00:50:54,833 --> 00:50:56,000
Thank you.

1134
00:50:57,416 --> 00:50:59,124
And your father?

1135
00:50:59,125 --> 00:51:03,999
I didn't know him,
and I had no other family, so...

1136
00:51:04,000 --> 00:51:07,333
went into foster care
and got out of there at 16.

1137
00:51:12,583 --> 00:51:14,625
She's my mom.

1138
00:51:18,000 --> 00:51:20,457
I didn't get enough time with her.

1139
00:51:20,458 --> 00:51:21,999
Olive, I can't imagine.

1140
00:51:22,000 --> 00:51:23,832
That must have been so hard.

1141
00:51:23,833 --> 00:51:25,624
I was a unique kid.

1142
00:51:25,625 --> 00:51:28,582
I mean, I memorized the periodic table
just for fun.

1143
00:51:28,583 --> 00:51:30,332
- Mm, that's not--
- It is fun.

1144
00:51:30,333 --> 00:51:31,832
No one would give it a chance.

1145
00:51:31,833 --> 00:51:33,540
- Mm.
- When my mom died,

1146
00:51:33,541 --> 00:51:37,166
I lost the one person in the whole world
who believed in me.

1147
00:51:38,500 --> 00:51:42,416
When she was gone, I was just
completely alone until I met Anh.

1148
00:51:43,416 --> 00:51:45,333
That's why this is so important to you.

1149
00:51:46,083 --> 00:51:49,291
This fake dating, it's for Anh.

1150
00:51:51,583 --> 00:51:55,875
Yeah, she's the only family I have,
and I would do anything for her.

1151
00:51:57,000 --> 00:51:58,290
I should kiss you.

1152
00:51:58,291 --> 00:51:59,625
What?

1153
00:52:00,208 --> 00:52:01,957
Your friends, they're watching
from the balcony.

1154
00:52:01,958 --> 00:52:03,583
Yeah, I could-- Yeah.

1155
00:52:05,666 --> 00:52:07,958
- Oh... my God.
- Okay.

1156
00:52:11,750 --> 00:52:13,458
He's starving.

1157
00:52:25,583 --> 00:52:26,583
Okay.

1158
00:52:32,083 --> 00:52:33,540
Good night.

1159
00:52:33,541 --> 00:52:35,041
Uh, good night.

1160
00:52:42,541 --> 00:52:44,415
Dude, you're killing me.

1161
00:52:44,416 --> 00:52:46,582
Your "hanging from a wire" genes
are genetically modified.

1162
00:52:46,583 --> 00:52:48,332
You're not supposed to be good at this.

1163
00:52:48,333 --> 00:52:49,874
I thought
I might find you here.

1164
00:52:49,875 --> 00:52:52,000
Hi, Dr. Benton.

1165
00:52:53,083 --> 00:52:56,165
I'm so sorry that I left
the party abruptly.

1166
00:52:56,166 --> 00:52:59,457
I did not give a good explanation
of my work.

1167
00:52:59,458 --> 00:53:02,540
I know our meeting is Wednesday,
but I read your research.

1168
00:53:02,541 --> 00:53:04,499
And while it really does show
a lot of promise--

1169
00:53:04,500 --> 00:53:06,999
You don't want to fund it.
You don't have to explain. It's fine.

1170
00:53:07,000 --> 00:53:08,582
No.

1171
00:53:08,583 --> 00:53:10,499
I need to hear more about the specifics.

1172
00:53:10,500 --> 00:53:13,874
What techniques you're using,
what challenges you foresee.

1173
00:53:13,875 --> 00:53:15,749
When you have the proper time to prepare.

1174
00:53:15,750 --> 00:53:17,749
Okay. Well, thank you so much, Dr. Benton.

1175
00:53:17,750 --> 00:53:20,374
I can present something to you
later this week.

1176
00:53:20,375 --> 00:53:23,500
I was actually thinking of something
on a little larger scale.

1177
00:53:24,125 --> 00:53:26,333
- Will you walk with me?
- Yes.

1178
00:53:28,916 --> 00:53:31,082
So, I am on the board
of the SBD conference

1179
00:53:31,083 --> 00:53:33,499
- scheduled in a couple weeks.
- Mm-hmm.

1180
00:53:33,500 --> 00:53:35,874
I would love for you to present
your abstract there.

1181
00:53:35,875 --> 00:53:37,915
Stop.

1182
00:53:37,916 --> 00:53:40,165
The Society for Biological Development
conference?

1183
00:53:40,166 --> 00:53:43,124
- That's the one.
- Oh, my God. That's huge.

1184
00:53:43,125 --> 00:53:44,665
That's huge.

1185
00:53:44,666 --> 00:53:47,290
That's for people
with extra letters behind their names.

1186
00:53:47,291 --> 00:53:50,040
I don't have those extra letters yet.

1187
00:53:50,041 --> 00:53:51,374
My friends and I are going.

1188
00:53:51,375 --> 00:53:54,249
We're just going to listen
to the people speak.

1189
00:53:54,250 --> 00:53:56,624
Well, I'll be able to hear
your entire proposal.

1190
00:53:56,625 --> 00:53:59,082
- Okay.
- In its proper context.

1191
00:53:59,083 --> 00:54:02,790
- Okay.
- I... consider it part of my apology

1192
00:54:02,791 --> 00:54:05,374
- for putting you on the spot.
- Oh, no. It's okay.

1193
00:54:05,375 --> 00:54:07,415
You're fine.

1194
00:54:07,416 --> 00:54:09,415
- So, are you in?
- Yes. Oh, my God, yes.

1195
00:54:09,416 --> 00:54:11,249
Thank you so much.

1196
00:54:11,250 --> 00:54:14,540
I feel like I have to ask:

1197
00:54:14,541 --> 00:54:17,083
Is this only happening
because I'm Adam's girlfriend?

1198
00:54:17,958 --> 00:54:19,957
- Hardly.
- Okay.

1199
00:54:19,958 --> 00:54:22,874
You know, back in our undergrad days
at Harvard,

1200
00:54:22,875 --> 00:54:24,375
I was known as the mean one.

1201
00:54:24,958 --> 00:54:28,916
Well, you can shove those tissue samples
up your ass, because I quit!

1202
00:54:30,500 --> 00:54:31,500
Nice.

1203
00:54:33,875 --> 00:54:35,207
Hey.

1204
00:54:35,208 --> 00:54:38,665
Hi. Um, Tom...

1205
00:54:38,666 --> 00:54:40,540
Let me guess. Rain check on lunch?

1206
00:54:40,541 --> 00:54:41,624
Yeah. You mind?

1207
00:54:41,625 --> 00:54:44,915
A spot just unexpectedly opened up
on my research team.

1208
00:54:44,916 --> 00:54:46,749
Olive, always a pleasure.

1209
00:54:46,750 --> 00:54:47,832
Thank you so much.

1210
00:54:47,833 --> 00:54:50,374
I look forward to hearing you present
at the conference.

1211
00:54:50,375 --> 00:54:51,665
Yeah.

1212
00:54:51,666 --> 00:54:53,416
Good luck finding a replacement, buddy.

1213
00:54:56,000 --> 00:54:57,374
You're speaking at the SBD conference?

1214
00:54:57,375 --> 00:55:00,915
Yes, if I don't have a stroke first,
which is likely.

1215
00:55:00,916 --> 00:55:02,416
Wow.

1216
00:55:04,166 --> 00:55:06,999
Hey, you know, your research students
may not quit all the time

1217
00:55:07,000 --> 00:55:09,832
if you tried being a tiny bit nicer.

1218
00:55:09,833 --> 00:55:12,416
You could show them a softer side of you.

1219
00:55:13,458 --> 00:55:14,874
Adam Slightly Less Evil.

1220
00:55:14,875 --> 00:55:16,332
Okay, smart-ass.

1221
00:55:16,333 --> 00:55:18,791
And how exactly do you suggest
I go about doing that?

1222
00:55:19,833 --> 00:55:21,249
You could come out with us tonight.

1223
00:55:21,250 --> 00:55:22,457
There's a party.

1224
00:55:22,458 --> 00:55:24,582
Okay? Anh's gonna be there.
We can look couple-y.

1225
00:55:24,583 --> 00:55:25,916
Mm.

1226
00:55:27,416 --> 00:55:29,915
I went to Dr. Fastuca's with you.

1227
00:55:29,916 --> 00:55:31,416
Okay.

1228
00:55:33,291 --> 00:55:34,790
- I'll come.
- Thank you.

1229
00:55:34,791 --> 00:55:36,749
♪ Lose control...

1230
00:55:36,750 --> 00:55:38,457
Drunk or high?

1231
00:55:38,458 --> 00:55:40,457
- We're making teams: drunk or high?
- High!

1232
00:55:40,458 --> 00:55:41,749
Ava, Ava.

1233
00:55:41,750 --> 00:55:43,124
- Drunk or high?
- Very drunk.

1234
00:55:43,125 --> 00:55:44,499
High, obviously.

1235
00:55:44,500 --> 00:55:46,207
Even better. Get in there.

1236
00:55:46,208 --> 00:55:48,790
- Drunk or high?
- No way.

1237
00:55:48,791 --> 00:55:50,333
Okay, lock in.

1238
00:55:52,000 --> 00:55:53,374
- Hi.
- Hi.

1239
00:55:53,375 --> 00:55:55,957
I did not believe
you would actually show up.

1240
00:55:55,958 --> 00:55:58,415
Well, I guess I'm just, uh,
full of surprises.

1241
00:55:58,416 --> 00:56:00,166
Yes, you are, aren't you?

1242
00:56:01,916 --> 00:56:04,624
- You want to play?
- Oh, no. I'm fine.

1243
00:56:04,625 --> 00:56:06,374
Come on, it's fun.

1244
00:56:06,375 --> 00:56:07,707
